<RaiseFault name="RF-ResponseBlocked">
  <DisplayName>403 — model response blocked by Model Armor</DisplayName>
  <!--
    The response-flow twin of RF-PromptBlocked, and it exists for the same
    reason: SMR-SanitizeModelResponse runs continueOnError="true" to survive a
    Model Armor outage, which would otherwise also swallow a genuine match and
    hand the caller content our own safety template just flagged.

    403 rather than 502 deliberately. A 5xx invites the client's retry/backoff
    path, and both repos retry 5xx — but re-issuing the same prompt will
    produce the same flagged output, so a retryable code turns one block into a
    retry loop that burns quota and Model Armor QPM for a guaranteed-identical
    result. 4xx is a definitive refusal, which is what this is.

    Same UNVERIFIED ASSUMPTION as RF-PromptBlocked: this reads filterMatchState
    on the error path and that has only been observed on clean calls. Prove it
    with a deliberately unsafe generation in a debug session before treating
    response blocking as real.
  -->
  <FaultResponse>
    <Set>
      <StatusCode>403</StatusCode>
      <ReasonPhrase>Forbidden</ReasonPhrase>
      <Payload contentType="application/json">{"error":"response_blocked","reason":"content_policy"}</Payload>
    </Set>
  </FaultResponse>
  <IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>true</IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>
</RaiseFault>
